WEBVTT

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.800
Speaker 1: Nigeria wasn't alone.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:27.520
Speaker 1: Across Africa, from Zionist churches in southern Africa to Rojo movements in Kenya, indigenous expressions of Christianity flourished.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.680
Speaker 1: These churches were not translations of European faith.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.920
Speaker 1: They were African transformations.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.680
Speaker 1: And while the Catholic and Anglican churches remained powerful, these numbers spoke clearly.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:51.600
Speaker 1: In Nigeria alone, Christianity grew from 22% in 1953 to 44.9% by the 1970s.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:01:00.890
Speaker 1: The faith was now being spoken in African tongues, sung in African rhythms, and lived in African ways.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:10.650
Speaker 1: The Pentecostal Fellowship of Nigeria is the national umbrella organization for Pentecostal churches in Nigeria.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:17.980
Speaker 1: It was founded in 1985 after Reverend Uma Okbai's Greater Lagos Crusade of thanks.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:29.170
Speaker 1: That year, the overwhelming Turnout, more than 23,000 prayer volunteers, gave birth to something bigger than the crusade itself.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:35.410
Speaker 1: What began as prayer support soon metamorphosed into a national body.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:47.420
Speaker 1: PFN was formally inaugurated on 14th November 1986 in Lagos and legally incorporated in June 1989.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:54.220
Speaker 1: Since then, it has continued to grow as a unifying voice for Pentecostals across the country.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:06.140
Speaker 1: Today, PFN's headquarters is in Isolo, Lagos, and its current national president is Bishop Francis Wale Oke, elected in 2021.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.890
Speaker 1: PFN was conceived and led by Nigeria's foremost Pentecostal pioneers.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:26.090
Speaker 1: Reverend Dr. Uma Okbai, whose 1985 crusade catalyzed its formation, stood alongside key figures like Pastor enoch Adeboye of RCCG, Pastor W.F.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:35.380
Speaker 1: Kumuyi of Deeper Life, Bishop Benson Idahosa of Church of God Mission, Bishop Mike Okonkwo of Trinity, Rev.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.874
Speaker 1: Charles Osweke of Assemblies of God Nigeria, Rev.

00:02:39.966 --> 00:02:48.260
Speaker 1: Dr. Obiora Ezekiel of CPMI, Pastor Elijah Abina of Government, Rev.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.700
Speaker 1: Wilson Badejo of Foursquare, Rev.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.220
Speaker 1: Daniel Ognkomi of Christ Gospel Mission, Rev.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.980
Speaker 1: Sam Okegbo of Faith Cathedral and Rev.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.090
Speaker 1: Abraham Oyeniro of Evangelical International.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:17.570
Speaker 1: Around this same period, a young Bishop David Oyedigbo began to emerge, planting what would later become the Living Faith Church worldwide, better known as Wenner's Chapel.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:32.080
Speaker 1: His rise alongside PFN's growing influence added a new voice to Nigeria's Pentecostal movement, shaping its theology, its education, and even its architecture of faith.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:39.040
Speaker 1: PFN was never born to exist in isolation alongside Pentecostals.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:54.390
Speaker 1: Nigeria's Christian landscape still flourishes, with Anglican, Methodist, Catholic, Baptist and other historic denominations each contributing to the rich mosaic of Christian witness in the nation.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:04:10.470
Speaker 1: When you hear names like James Boyejo, Enoch Adeboye, Benson Idahosa, Mike Okonkwo, Ayo Orise, Jafor, Felix Omobude Francis Waleoke.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.950
Speaker 1: All of these men of God have been PFN presidents at some point.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:21.269
Speaker 1: Although from diverse Pentecostal denominations, they became united under the pfn.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.190
Speaker 1: But this path was not without pain.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:30.710
Speaker 1: From the late 1970s, Nigeria's religious tensions flared.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:38.390
Speaker 1: By 1982, violence exploded in Kano as Christians and Muslims clashed.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:46.950
Speaker 1: Then came the Kafanchan crisis, where 152 churches and five mosques were set ablaze.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.960
Speaker 1: The message was chilling.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.040
Speaker 1: Peace was fragile.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:06.000
Speaker 1: A 2023 Vatican news report revealed that between 2009 and 2022, over 5 million Nigerian Christians were displaced, pushed into IDP camps across the country.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:13.200
Speaker 1: At least 52,250 Christians were murdered by Islamist militants.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:19.470
Speaker 1: Over 18,000 churches and 2,200 schools were torched.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:24.750
Speaker 1: And approximately 34,000 moderate Muslims were also killed.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.030
Speaker 1: The names echo in our conscience.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:37.310
Speaker 1: Deborah Samuel Yakubo, stoned and burned alive in Sokoto on May 12, 2022, for blasphemy.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:44.740
Speaker 1: Eunice Olawale, a street preacher, murdered while evangelizing in Abuja in 2016.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.980
Speaker 1: With independence came a new fire.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.740
Speaker 1: African Christians were no longer the evangelized.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.900
Speaker 1: They were the evangelists.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:03.540
Speaker 1: This reverse mission has seen Africans planting churches across Europe, Asia and the Americas.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:08.850
Speaker 1: In England, the largest church is led by a Nigerian.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:16.130
Speaker 1: In Ukraine, the largest congregation is pastored by guess who, a Nigerian, of course.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:24.690
Speaker 1: Nigeria's Redeemed Christian Church of God and Ghana's Church of Pentecost now have branches across the globe.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:36.980
Speaker 1: Social media and technology have amplified their reach, allowing sermons or worship and prayer services to be broadcast instantly across nations.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:44.500
Speaker 1: Africa's youthful population means that this movement continues to grow at a rapid pace.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.980
Speaker 1: Christianity in Africa today has moved beyond survival to expansion.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:07:05.980
Speaker 1: Universities established by churches like Covenant University, Redeemers University, Babcock University, and so on testify to the role of the church in shaping education worldwide.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:11.980
Speaker 1: Christianity remains the largest faith, with about 2.6 billion adherents.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.100
Speaker 1: That's about one third of the world's population.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:29.390
Speaker 1: From the prayer houses of Ibadan to the cathedrals of Europe, and from house fellowships in Asia to stadium crusades in the Americas, the church remains alive and multiplying.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:42.190
Speaker 1: And in just 12 years, Celebration Church International, one of Nigeria's youngest megachurches, now has over 33 campuses across three continents.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:49.790
Speaker 1: What began in village missions and colonial schoolhouses has become a global movement.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:55.740
Speaker 1: Christianity in Nigeria has walked through fire and come out as gold.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:06.540
Speaker 1: Despite challenges and persecution in Africa, and indeed across the world since its inception, the words of Jesus remain true.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:13.340
Speaker 1: I will build my church and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:23.920
Speaker 1: The followers of Jesus and the faith they profess will continue to remain and multiply in leaps and bounds until he returns.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.760
Speaker 1: Now the world listens when Africa sings the gospel.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.240
Speaker 1: Once carried by foreign feet, now dances in African rhythm.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:42.799
Speaker 1: From Ogerre to Oxford, from Kavanchan to Kyiv, the evangelized have become the evangelists.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:50.540
Speaker 1: It.
